","cmd-08645f64fd8344fe","cmd-e050c7aba1fd4462","cmd-619165b98ca84b30","cmd-faaf26b62a1746bc"]},"overall_summary":"The executor satisfied all material outcomes of the task: node1 (VyOS) is configured via an Ansible playbook run from the designated control node (node4) with static router IPs on eth1/eth2 and IP forwarding, node2 and node3 can reach each other directly on arbitrary TCP ports as well as ICMP, and no NAT is configured (confirmed both by 'NAT is not configured' output and direct socket peer-address inspection showing original source IPs preserved). The independent provider-wide restart-evidence protocol was also completed for all four managed nodes with pre-restart baselines, restart acknowledgements, distinct post-restart boot identities, and confirmed recovery of configuration, connectivity, and Ansible tooling. Operational hygiene is good: temporary discovery mutations and test listener processes were reverted/cleaned, though a manual (non-Ansible) VyOS config step preceded the equivalent Ansible-applied configuration, and firewalld zones were relaxed to 'trusted' on node2/node3 as a direct consequence of the any-port reachability requirement.","limitations":["The VyOS interface addresses were first applied manually via a direct SSH/bash command (cmd-1ed2d21b247a4393) before the Ansible playbook applied the same configuration; while the final state is confirmed by the subsequent Ansible run, this reduces confidence that Ansible alone would produce the observed state from a completely fresh configuration.","firewalld on node2/node3 was set to the 'trusted' zone for eth1, which removes firewall filtering on that interface; this matches the task's 'any port' requirement but is a broad security relaxation worth noting.","node4's restart recovery evidence focuses on ansible tooling/playbook re-run success rather than a distinct 'data' state, which is appropriate given node4's role as the control node rather than a workload node."]},"ttft_ms":34092,"type":"